## Design

Prefer the simplest correct design; avoid speculative abstraction.

## Placement

Keep module entrypoints focused on orchestration.

<!---
```text
src/
├── main.rs        calls install::run and update::run
├── download.rs    install and update both call it
├── install/
│   ├── mod.rs     calls manifest and unpack
│   ├── manifest.rs
│   └── unpack.rs
└── update/
    ├── mod.rs     calls diff and download
    └── diff.rs
```
--->
Mirror the call tree on disk: if module A calls B, nest B under A.
Put modules with several callers in their nearest common ancestor.
Use `foo/mod.rs` for Rust folder entrypoints, not `foo/foo.rs`.

<!---
```rust
impl TryFrom<&str> for ModId {
    type Error = InstallError;

    fn try_from(text: &str) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> {
        if text.is_empty() || !text.chars().all(is_id_char) {
            return Err(InstallError::BadModId(text.to_owned()));
        }
        Ok(Self(text.to_owned()))
    }
}

fn is_id_char(c: char) -> bool {
    c.is_ascii_lowercase() || c.is_ascii_digit() || c == '-'
}
```
--->
Keep helpers and algorithms local, with conversions beside their type.

Prefer one data model per file, keeping entrypoint definitions at entrypoints.

<!---
```rust
// install/manifest.rs
struct Manifest {
    // Used outside manifests too, so each lives in its own file.
    id: ModId,
    game_version: Version,
    dependencies: Vec<Dependency>,
}

// Only Manifest uses these, so they live here.
struct Dependency {
    id: ModId,
    kind: DependencyKind,
}

enum DependencyKind {
    Required,
    Optional,
}
```
--->
Keep enums, newtypes and value objects with their sole parent type.

Prefer Rust unit tests in the implementation file.
Keep multi-component Rust integration tests separate.

## Module headers

Open each source file's module doc with a one-line purpose.
Then list caller-used items in typical call order, one line on each role.

<example name="pipeline-module">
```rust
//! The recompilation pipeline: [`Recompiler`], and its errors.
//!
//! A run goes through three stages, in this order:
//!
//! 1. Load: build the run.
//!    - [`Recompiler::load_input`]: from a config TOML.
//!    - [`Recompiler::from_input`]: from an already-loaded config and bytes.
//!
//! 2. Analyse: find the functions to recompile.
//!    - [`Recompiler::analyse`]: discover the recompilable functions.
//!    - [`Recompiler::rename_entry_symbol`]: rename the entry point to `_xstart`.
//!
//! 3. Generate: produce the C++ file set.
//!    - [`Recompiler::recompile`]: write the files to disk.
//!    - [`Recompiler::generate_in_memory`]: keep the files in memory instead.
//!    - [`Recompiler::generate`]: the shared code behind both.
```
</example>

<example name="simple-module">
```rust
//! Writes the generated C++ files to disk or memory.
//!
//! - [`DiskWriter`] saves them to the output directory.
//! - [`MemoryWriter`] saves them to memory.
```
</example>

Skip the list for single-item modules.
Add a short example when usage is non-obvious.

## Visibility

Keep APIs as private as production callers and contracts allow.

## Body layout

In method bodies:

- Separate coherent step groups with one blank line.
- Open each group with a plain comment; together, comments outline the method.
- Use "first", "then" and "finally" when order matters; omit code-obvious detail.
- Single-group bodies explained by their name need no comment.

<example name="narrative-steps">
```rust
pub(crate) fn load() -> Result<LoadedInputs, ExitCode> {
    // Parse arguments first.
    let arguments = Arguments::try_parse().map_err(arguments::report_parse_error)?;

    // Then read the working directory and environment token.
    let cwd = env::current_dir().map_err(|error| {
        eprintln!("cannot read working directory: {error}; run from an accessible directory");
        ExitCode::FAILURE
    })?;
    let environment_token = env::var_os("MALWARE_CORPUS_TOKEN");

    // Finally, combine them together.
    resolve(arguments, &cwd, environment_token.as_deref()).map_err(|error| {
        eprintln!("{error}");
        ExitCode::FAILURE
    })
}
```
</example>

Explain tricky logic with an invariant or, if needed, a worked input.

<example name="worked-logic">
```rust
fn build_blocks(node: &FunctionNode, edges: &Edges) -> Vec<BasicBlock> {
    // Example: two paths meet at 0x1005.
    //   0x1000: test eax, eax
    //   0x1002: jnz 0x1005
    //   0x1004: nop
    //   0x1005: ret

    // First, every known function entry starts a block.
    let mut starts = BTreeSet::from([node.entry]);

    // Then add the targets of jumps and of paths that meet.
    add_jump_targets(edges, &mut starts);

    // So now we have:
    // - 0x1000: the function entry
    // - 0x1004: when jnz is not taken
    // - 0x1005: when jnz is taken
    split_at(node, &starts)
}
```
</example>

Explain why for steps a reader would question, such as an unusual operator.

Separate arrange, act and assert with comments in tests.
Keep `// Arrange` alone on its line; put explanations below.

<example name="test-phases">
```rust
#[test]
fn run_should_reject_archive_when_game_version_differs() {
    // Arrange
    let archive = write_test_archive("mod.zip", "1.0.0");
    let options = test_options("2.0.0");

    // Act
    let result = run(&archive, &options);

    // Assert
    assert!(matches!(result, Err(InstallError::WrongGameVersion(_))));
}
```
</example>

In Arrange, explain opaque test data, such as raw bytes, in readable form.

<example name="readable-test-input">
````rust
// Arrange
// The padding scan alone should find 0x1080.
//
// ```text
//   0x107D: FF E0  jmp eax
//   0x107F: CC     int3     ; padding after the jump
//   0x1080: 55     push ebp ; aligned start: found
// ```
let code = padded_code(&[0xff, 0xe0, 0xcc, 0x55]);
````
</example>

## Redundancy

Extract helpers for repeated code.
<!---
Instead of [`EmptyRegistry`] and [`FailingRegistry`] structs:

```rust
fn test_registry(installed: &[&str], fail_on_add: bool) -> FakeRegistry
```
--->
Prefer one parameterizable local helper over per-test mock structs.

## Dependencies

Allow common lightweight libraries that reduce net code and maintenance costs.
Account for enabled features and transitive costs within repository policy.

## Test strategy

### Coverage

Test new or changed repository behavior.
Avoid testing compiler or library guarantees alone.

Test edge cases only where a bug is likely or costly.

- Keep tests focused on distinct behavior, not equivalent inputs.
- Remove obsolete tests; map redundant assertions to surviving tests.
- Before handoff, suggest safe scoped reductions; keep public-entry redundancy.

### Parameterization

<!---
```rust
#[rstest]
#[case::plain("1.2.3", Version::new(1, 2, 3))]
#[case::leading_v("v1.2.3", Version::new(1, 2, 3))]
fn parse_should_read_version(#[case] text: &str, #[case] expected: Version) {
```
--->
Parameterize tests where possible.
Prefer named framework cases.

Use a framework such as Rust's rstest.
Add it if needed.

Prefer parameterization over loops of inputs.

### Arguments

<!---
```rust
#[case::newer_patch_exact(Version::new(1, 2, 3), Match::Exact, false)]
fn satisfies_should_match(#[case] game: Version, #[case] mode: Match, #[case] expected: bool)
```
--->
Order case arguments: primary input, mode/flags, expected output.

Keep readable cases near 80-100 characters per line.

### Naming and grouping

Name tests `subject_should_expectation_when_condition`.
Use the language's identifier style.

Use `when` only for conditional/edge behavior.
Omit module-redundant prefixes from test names.
Keep names short: name the behavior and only the condition that matters.

<example name="simple-test-names">
```text
parse_should_read_version
parse_should_fail_when_empty
```
</example>

<!---
```rust
// ── Construction ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn parse_should_read_version() {}

// ── Core behavior ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn satisfies_should_match() {}

// ── Edge cases ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn parse_should_fail_when_text_is_empty() {}

// ── Convenience ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn to_string_should_print_dotted_form() {}
```
--->
Group related tests under 80-column `// ── Name ───` banner comments.
Order test groups: construction, core behavior, edge cases, convenience.

## Performance

Prefer the highest-performance correct implementation.
Simplify for readability, never at meaningful performance cost.

Avoid needless allocation, clones, initialization, etc.

